---
// An index for /notes/, which used to answer 403.
//
// A replication publishes as its own note rather than as an instrument — /contribute/
// says so, and the distinction is deliberate: an instrument is a pipeline, models, an
// evaluation and an interface, while a replication is a finding. But the consequence
// was that the only finished interactive work on the site sat two levels down behind a
// path with no index, reachable from one link inside one table cell. Somebody asked
// "no dashboard?" and they were right to.
//
// The count and the list are derived from the files in this directory, so a new note
// appears here without anyone remembering to add it — every hand-kept count on this
// site went stale at least once. The cascade pair gets hand-written copy because a
// meta description is not a summary; anything else falls through to the generic list,
// which is what stops this page going out of date.
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import G from "../../data/cascade-cold.json";

const failed = G.gates.filter((x) => x.status === "fail").length;
const passed = G.gates.filter((x) => x.status === "pass").length;

const pages = Object.entries(
  import.meta.glob("./*.astro", { eager: true, query: "?raw", import: "default" }),
)
  .filter(([path]) => !path.endsWith("/index.astro"))
  .map(([path, src]) => {
    const slug = path.replace(/^\.\//, "").replace(/\.astro$/, "");
    const title = /title="([^"]+)"/.exec(src)?.[1] ?? slug;
    const description = /description="([^"]+)"/.exec(src)?.[1] ?? "";
    return { slug, title, description };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => a.slug.localeCompare(b.slug));

const NOTE = pages.find((p) => p.slug === "cascade-refrigeration");
const EXPL = pages.find((p) => p.slug === "cascade-explorer");
// anything that is not the cascade note or its dashboard renders from its own frontmatter
const others = pages.filter((p) => p !== NOTE && p !== EXPL);
---

<Page
  title="Notes — replications and findings"
  description="Replications published as notes rather than instruments: a published method rebuilt from the paper alone, and what reproduced."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Notes · {pages.length} published</p>
    <h1>Replications publish as notes,<br /><em>not as instruments.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An instrument is a pipeline, models, an evaluation and an interface, built to answer
      a question nobody has answered. A replication is a different thing: somebody has
      already answered it, in print, and the question is whether the answer can be
      rebuilt from what they published. That is a finding, so it publishes as a note.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      A note can still carry a dashboard. The one below does, and it recomputes the whole
      operating envelope in your browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="published">
    <div class="rail">Published<span>what each one found</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">

      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">A cascade refrigeration optimisation · Makara J. Technol. 20(3), 2016</p>
        <h2><a href={`/notes/${NOTE.slug}/`}>{passed} of {passed + failed} checks
          reproduced. {failed === 1 ? "One did" : "Two did"} not.</a></h2>
        <p>
          A published two-objective optimisation of a −80&nbsp;°C cascade system, rebuilt
          from the paper's own equations and re-scored against the numbers it reports. It
          needed nothing from the authors — no dataset, no code, no permission. Three
          things the paper never stated had to be reconstructed, and one of them, the
          compressor efficiency, was recovered from the paper's own reported COP.
        </p>
        <dl class="tally">
          <div><dt>Checks passed</dt><dd>{passed}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Checks failed</dt><dd class="want">{failed}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>From the authors</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Points in the dashboard</dt><dd>62,370</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="go">
          <a href={`/notes/${NOTE.slug}/`}>Read the note<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
          <a class="strong" href={`/notes/${EXPL.slug}/`}>Open the dashboard<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        </p>
      </article>

      {others.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="more">
          {others.map((n) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`/notes/${n.slug}/`}>{n.title}</a>
              {n.description && <span>{n.description}</span>}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>Bring one.</b> A replication needs no data and no code from the original
          authors — only a paper whose method is described well enough to rebuild. It is
          the fastest way to contribute here, and the result is a finding either way:
          reproducing a published result is as publishable as failing to.
          <a href="/contribute/#attack">How that works.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }
  .sub { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }

  .card { border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--panel);
    padding: 1.5rem 1.6rem; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; }
  .kicker { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .card h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .card h2 a { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .card h2 a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .card > p { max-width: 68ch; }

  .tally {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 8rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1px; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding: 0;
    background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .tally > div { background: var(--ground); padding: 0.8rem 0.95rem; }
  .tally dt { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.64rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); }
  .tally dd { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600; line-height: 1; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: var(--ink); }
  .tally dd.want { color: var(--flag); text-shadow: 0 0 22px var(--glow-flag); }

  .go { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.6rem 1.8rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .go a { color: var(--muted); text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 2px; }
  .go a:hover { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  .go a.strong { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600; }

  .more { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .more li { padding: 1rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .more a { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .more a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .more span { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--muted); max-width: 68ch; }
</style>
